Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Changing Magnetic Flux", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->CompressedData["
1:eJxTTMoPSmViYGCQAmIQvejASQaRRW8dr7++wQiiHwcpzpgGpKUMf80E0eFt
PNkzgPQjf8EcEH1shsxZED2nhe8ciD5amO05E0grZDN5g+iFE4/n+S9+65hm
fw9MJ3YdFA4E0nzMt8E0U/Hy5ZlAuuTROjAtd+XYahA9q7ZnA4hecn/9FhD9
QekrmM7g3P4BRN9LufwdRAeI3wnPBtJWcs/BtF0c14VNQLrs/TMwrZF88wqI
Xi7MfxVEX3PpY3i65K2jztdERhC9x+qgMYjO0VhqAqLFZWpVngHpL5Ui6iC6
jGGDHYi+UyZuD6IFykO2gulTB8H0wZSlEs+B9FlmFUkQPeu7jRaIviw8RRtE
p3jmmoDoU0mzwPScGMscEB23YzmYbv+T582/7K3jJ41GMK1182ux94q3jiKT
z5SB6FJm8ff+QNqGxfsDiAYAy+XhDQ==
  "],ExpressionUUID->"0250aca7-8fd1-4d9c-9f7b-b0cc66530f42"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In the diagram in the previous section was the formula ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[CapitalPhi]", "=", 
    RowBox[{"B", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "A", " ", "cos", " ", "\[Theta]"}]}], 
   TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "a5f90823-7fcd-4eea-852a-8a84ee9447fc"],
 ". This formula is only valid for a magnetic field that has a constant \
direction, and only for a flat area, and I will spare you the generalization, \
which requires multivariable calculus.\n\nThe area drawn is imaginary! We \
only imagine how much magnetic field passes through it. We might often ask \
when doing the calculation, how much field the area \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]catches,\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] but of course, since \
it is imaginary, it actually does no catching.\n\nThe ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"cos", " ", "\[Theta]"}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "6f0efe3a-f9a1-4d7d-875e-702a7e06e7ff"],
 " in the formula is plausible. It says that if the area \
isn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t oriented across the field, then it won\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t catch as much of the field going through it. If you \
turned the area completely sideways (by ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"90", "\.ba"}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "f825f2d9-d3af-498f-bf62-d4c2a8a9366a"],
 "), the area wouldn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t catch any field and this comports \
with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"cos", " ", "90", "\[Degree]"}], "=", "0."}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "ca432a3c-612f-4d86-925f-05b712dd3ff1"],
 " If the angle shown in the diagram is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"0", "\[Degree]"}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "5ef11445-39b5-4218-84b5-a7aeb37afc25"],
 ", the area is catching as much field as it can, and this comports with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"cos", " ", "0", "\[Degree]"}], "=", "1"}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "73f857b4-f101-42e5-b6b0-20386441a653"],
 " (because 1 is the maximum value of the cosine.\n\nIt turns out that flux \
has little effect, unless it is changing! There are lots of ways to make flux \
change. We could imagine that the area is rotating so that sometimes ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[Theta]", "=", 
    RowBox[{"0", "\[Degree]"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "ec0c58a6-f647-4240-b4bd-c4c6d9f910cb"],
 ", a little later it is 90\[Degree], then 180\[Degree], and then \
270\[Degree], before getting back to 360\[Degree] which is equivalent to 0\
\[Degree].\n\nAnother way to change the flux is to have the magnetic field \
increase and decrease in strength. Mims set up a crude version of changing \
flux by changing the strength of a magnetic field this by having you having \
you press a switch that connected and disconnected a current going to the \
transformer windings. We\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll see more about what Mims did \
shortly."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->CompressedData["
1:eJwdxWkoQwEAB/A5c2RyhWjRkqwYig/OPUU0t0TuhIhchVHk+kTKkSsrqTeE
OWObhN7krFma5JGIXJkrhTK8/z78+rnnVyQXGrJYLHcG/hrXRdySWuLTeC4X
b2yzS7FTqJt++t6qSv9uTCuOPLHpxLeD0T249i9ThqngrnvMbjF5xmaiGfM7
5tl0PzaO3KuzxzMWZhzs6pkagDVtglp8zhloxavXCT2417tIjI/pnAX8bde8
jIsPpqXW41qi2yBqHu9H5cswRTQp8E2/sUg4qSXEP2F92JQ/O4Q/zmpGMTuO
JHHGRIEEL0nKpFjg7LiAd2qEMpwT30BhcsBhOIHZt9JyHmt4I3LcKGpRY7k/
j8blK9wrnD0hfMDxRMgTnpIq3vFWigknkXld5eGDL7rzQnDH4rYAc+n6WDxp
kavEtmtV+g3H+CosOBKpcVYJpcENm+QJfngTn+ESudcLvnz90uGgtKZf/KiU
GiUx845OXXA7P1CIq8O9svHUh2QNiw+VFP4Hjpoikw==
  "],ExpressionUUID->"40d3abfc-9bc6-4bcb-8145-31520cd66f22"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Effect of Changing Magnetic Flux \[LongDash] Faraday\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s Law", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->CompressedData["
1:eJwlxzFIQlEYQOGn1lCEFk+wRCRDSKylcklEe9BiDqYYLiFoFoVmhGU0OBRR
EFFEESoumWYQpERSQ0MOGUlGoFmSGBTpEA8lEiEauv9tOHwckWVGP84kCEKA
AgNXtwQ3QFNPn88M8F0v8uwh+T0/XtC42mTzIN+0LXYw4RGkQP8K+x68nrWp
vch2G1MD7m/fOLQHNDWhKmDN63FSh2SzXrBMZzg8hZx7O8EKM4lj0OfeiILB
18gZWOmoYicbzitgwZqugcO8vNGGlAtLWKWp8eEU6SoXsZKxXAYMk5xHMDu4
SXwEaaq7amaAl/J4H2iXhGQgT+AWF5Hfi9xO0EVElWDexVOBzQuGGDYZx8at
odYSMsUSt4G+mkIKpsndLtCqnpaBSYsP6x/tt4OmizB27deh4RzS1JdkCSvN
VZ2aI5ri7ty5wHkWr6xFKuo0FXCZP5DVwQ9t/UtGDHpkfax3BPwD52nv3Q==

  "],ExpressionUUID->"5aea4dd9-2de1-40c3-934d-27cda56ad831"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Remarkably a voltage is generated when magnetic flux changes. Imagine \
putting a wire with resistance, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["R", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "f3b1ec05-b221-4ea8-9371-eaced492dd54"],
 ", around the area, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["A", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "d47b9f41-0657-4eaa-9ccb-2ad591d7234d"],
 ", and causing the flux through the area to change at a rate ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   FractionBox[
    RowBox[{"d", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalPhi]"}], 
    RowBox[{"d", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "t"}]], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "d53815fe-2a91-406b-864e-43777573ab04"],
 ". Then a current will be generated in the wire:\n\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"I", "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"-", 
      FractionBox["1", "R"]}], 
     FractionBox[
      RowBox[{"d", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalPhi]"}], 
      RowBox[{"d", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "t"}]]}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "8cf699ce-26d7-4b3c-9c9e-dfcc95c73908"],
 "\n\nI\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll spare you the reason for the minus sign for a \
moment, and instead concentrate on the fact that if we multiply through by ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["R", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "48af5bab-2587-49b2-83a2-8ec44bcf0359"],
 ", we have:\n\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"I", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "R"}], "=", 
    RowBox[{"-", 
     FractionBox[
      RowBox[{"d", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalPhi]"}], 
      RowBox[{"d", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "t"}]]}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "8fd3b4db-0216-4f2f-9bcf-d0275571a7ab"],
 "\n\n",
 StyleBox["This suggests that there is a voltage in the wire going around a \
circle, and that makes a mockery of Kirchoff\[CloseCurlyQuote]s loop rule \
which said that the sum of the voltages around any loop is zero.",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "\n\nWe\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll still use Kirchoff\[CloseCurlyQuote]s loop rule \
unchanged but we will have to incorporate transformers and inductors into it \
quite carefully. So you usually see:\n\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[ScriptCapitalE]", "=", 
    RowBox[{"-", 
     FractionBox[
      RowBox[{"d", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalPhi]"}], 
      RowBox[{"d", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "t"}]]}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "8f0384c6-ec98-483e-a389-d499da782518"],
 "\n\nwhere \[ScriptCapitalE] is the voltage in the loop. This is Faraday\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s Law of Induction."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.980562103316793*^9, 3.980562200459537*^9}, {
   3.980562444896723*^9, 3.9805626396241827`*^9}, {3.980562741929781*^9, 
   3.980562919280633*^9}, {3.980563040275832*^9, 3.9805631054053802`*^9}, 
   3.980563149617508*^9, {3.980563195143515*^9, 3.980563195354033*^9}, {
   3.980566163084282*^9, 3.9805661633951483`*^9}, {3.9805662055912323`*^9, 
   3.980566205876707*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"501aa3e4-7436-4010-acfa-\
ee8da467c9be"]
}, Open  ]],

